<h1>how do I know if a job posting is actually entry level or fake</h1>

<p class="answer-capsule">Most "entry level" postings are confidence theater: they list three years of experience and a master's degree because HR copied the last req, not because the role demands it. The real filter is whether the posting asks for proof of skill or just proof of survival.</p>

<div class="tldr">
  <ul>
    <li>46% of employers dropped degree requirements for some roles, but only 31% followed through with real change, and fewer than 1 in 700 hires reflected that shift, <a href="https://www.burningglassinstitute.org/research/skills-based-hiring-2024">Burning Glass Institute + HBS</a>. The gap between posted policy and actual practice is where fake entry-level jobs live.</li>
    <li>53% of employers claim to have dropped degree requirements, up from 30% the year before, <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">TestGorilla State of Skills-Based Hiring 2025</a>. But a posted "no degree required" without a skills-based screening process often means the req was never updated.</li>
    <li>Resume use fell to 67% in 2025, down from 73%, <a href="https://www.testgorilla.com/skills-based-hiring/state-of-skills-based-hiring-2025/">same TestGorilla report</a>. Employers that actually moved to skills-based hiring replaced the resume with a work sample or scored task. If the posting still demands a resume plus three years, the "entry level" label is decoration.</li>
    <li>New-grad hiring at big tech is down more than 50% from 2019; new grads are now roughly 7% of hires, <a href="https://www.signalfire.com/blog/signalfire-state-of-talent-report-2025">SignalFire State of Talent Report 2025</a>. Some "entry level" postings exist to harvest resumes for future pipelines, not to fill current seats.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<section>
  <h2>why do companies post "entry level" jobs that require three years of experience?</h2>
  <p>The honest answer is laziness, not malice. Most job descriptions are recycled templates. The hiring manager writes "entry level" in the title because that's the budget band, then copies bullet points from the senior role because nobody has time to rewrite reqs. The result is a posting that asks for proficiency in tools the team barely uses and "proven track record" language that filters out anyone who actually is entry level.</p>
  <p>There is a darker version too: resume farming. Some postings exist to build a talent database for future quarters, to make the company look like it's growing to investors, or to satisfy internal rules that require "posting publicly" before backfilling internally. The "entry level" tag lowers the friction for applicants, maximizing the harvest.</p>
  <p>The filter OS at work again: the posting is designed to reduce applicant volume, not to find the right person. It asks for years survived instead of skill demonstrated, because years are easier to scan. The tragedy is that some of these roles <em>could</em> be genuinely entry level if the screening matched the label. The company just never bothered to build that screening.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>what specific signals tell you a posting is real?</h2>
  <p>Real entry-level postings describe outcomes, not credentials. Look for language about what you will <em>do</em> in the first 90 days, not what you must have <em>already</em> done. "You'll shadow two account managers and own your first client by month three" signals a structured ramp. "Proven ability to manage enterprise accounts" signals a copy-paste from a senior req.</p>
  <p>The screening mechanism is the clearest tell. Does the application ask for a resume and cover letter only, or does it move quickly into a work sample, a short task, a scored exercise? Employers that actually dropped degree requirements in practice, not just on paper, replaced the resume with something substantive. If you see "submit a 10-minute analysis of this dataset" or "record a 3-minute walkthrough of your approach," the posting is more likely to be genuine because the company invested in evaluation design.</p>
  <p>Another signal: who wrote it. Postings with a named hiring manager, a specific team mentioned, or a realistic reporting structure ("you'll report to the director of product and work alongside two other associates") tend to be real. Vague postings with no team context, no start date, no office location even for hybrid roles, and "we're always hiring" language are often pipeline builders, not live seats.</p>
  <p>Finally, check the date. A posting refreshed for 90 days without a "position filled" update is either stale or a perpetual harvester. Real entry-level roles fill fast because the candidate pool is large and the bar is, honestly, lower.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>how do you spot the ghost jobs that never intend to hire?</h2>
  <p>Ghost jobs are the natural output of a fragmented recruiting market. Companies post them to signal growth, to satisfy compliance, or to keep a warm bench of candidates for roles that may open someday. The "entry level" label is especially useful here because it attracts the widest funnel with the lowest friction.</p>
  <p>The red flags are consistent. The posting appears on every job board simultaneously with identical language, suggesting automated distribution rather than targeted search. The company has dozens of "entry level" postings across unrelated functions, all posted the same week, which rarely reflects real coordinated hiring. The description uses generic corporate language that could apply to any company in any industry: "fast-paced environment," "passionate self-starter," "make an impact."</p>
  <p>Most telling: the application process is frictionless. One-click apply, no questions about your actual work, no skills assessment, just a resume upload into a black hole. Real hiring has friction because evaluation is expensive, while ghost jobs optimize for volume, not discernment.</p>
  <p>You can verify before you apply. Search LinkedIn for people in that exact role at that company, with start dates in the last six months. If you find none, and the posting has been live for months, the role is likely imaginary. Check the company's careers page directly: sometimes the "entry level" posting exists on Indeed but not on their own site, which means a third-party aggregator invented or aged it.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>what do you do when the posting is fake but the company is real?</h2>
  <p>The old path says apply anyway, it's a numbers game. The honest path says: prove you can do the work and route around the posting entirely. A fake entry-level job is a symptom that the company's hiring process is broken, not that the company has no needs. Someone, somewhere, is understaffed and would value proof of skill over proof of patience with broken systems.</p>
  <p>This is where the degree-as-mortgage analogy bites hardest. You could spend four years building the credential the posting nominally wants, or you could spend four weeks building the proof that makes the posting irrelevant, because the mortgage buys you access to the filter while proof of work lets you bypass it.</p>
  <p>Find the hiring manager on LinkedIn, not HR. Send not a cover letter but a 48-hour project: "I noticed your team ships mobile updates weekly. I rebuilt your onboarding flow as a prototype and documented where I found friction. 3-minute walkthrough attached." The response rate is lower than spray-and-pray, but the hit rate on real conversations is an order of magnitude higher. You are no longer a resume in a ghost-job database. You are a person who shipped something.</p>
  <p>The companies that matter are moving, slowly, toward skills-based hiring. The ones that are not will keep posting fake entry-level jobs because the filter OS is all they know. Your move is to make the filter obsolete for you, not to optimize your chances inside it.</p>
</section>

<section>
  <h2>how should you change your search strategy?</h2>
  <p>Stop trusting job titles. "Entry level," "junior," "associate," "new grad": these are budget categories, not skill descriptors. A "senior" role at a startup often wants less credentialing than an "entry level" role at a Fortune 500 because the startup cannot afford to train and needs immediate proof. Read the requirements as if they were written by a tired person at 11 PM, because they were.</p>
  <p>Prioritize postings that describe the work sample or assessment upfront. This filters for companies that actually invested in evaluation, which correlates with actually intending to hire. Deprioritize postings with laundry lists of "preferred" qualifications that outnumber required ones. "Preferred" is HR's way of saying "we copied the senior role and added this word to make it legal."</p>
  <p>Build your search around problems, not titles. Identify companies doing work you can prove you can do. Follow their engineering blogs, product launches, customer complaints. Ship a relevant critique or improvement. The best entry-level job is often the one that was never posted, opened by a manager who saw your work and realized they had a gap.</p>
  <p>The Google ads algorithm for people does not exist yet in recruiting, but the logic does. Match yourself to roles by demonstrated fit, not by keyword alignment. The posting is a crude keyword match, while your proof of work is the precise match, if you can get it in front of the right human.</p>
</section>

<section class="faq">
  <h2>faq</h2>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>is "entry level" with a degree requirement automatically fake?</h3>
    <p>Not automatically, but suspect. A real entry-level role that requires a degree should explain why that specific credential matters for the work, not just list it as a default filter. Most degree requirements in "entry level" postings are legacy, not logic.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>should i apply to jobs that say "2-3 years preferred" if i have none?</h3>
    <p>If you have proof of equivalent skill, yes, and bypass the application portal if possible. "Preferred" is weaker than "required," but in practice it functions as a screen. Your counter is not to argue but to demonstrate.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>how do i tell if a company is really hiring or just collecting resumes?</h3>
    <p>Check for recent hires in the role on LinkedIn, look for a specific start date or hiring timeline in the posting, and see if the application includes substantive evaluation beyond resume upload. No recent hires plus perpetual posting plus frictionless apply equals resume farming.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>what if the only jobs i find are fake entry-level postings?</h3>
    <p>This is common in saturated markets. The correction is to shift from reactive application to proactive proof. Identify companies, build for them publicly, and route around the broken filter. The posting is a middleman you can often eliminate.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="faq-item">
    <h3>does zero help with this problem?</h3>
    <p>zero builds scored proof of work that recruiters can search and match against roles, bypassing the resume-and-posting theater. You do real tasks, get scored against a professional bar, and get matched to companies hiring for demonstrated skill, not credential keywords.</p>
  </div>
</section>

